{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And moreover she is indeed my sister, my father's daughter</b>. I do not know the reason for this justification. For even if the matter were true — that she was his sister and his wife, and when they desired the woman he said to them, \"She is my sister,\" in order to deceive them in this matter — he still sinned against them by bringing upon them a great sin, and there is no difference or distinction whatsoever between whether the matter is true or false. But perhaps, since Avimelekh said, \"What did you see that you did this thing?\" — meaning, what sin or wickedness did you see in me that you feared and did so, for I had no intention of taking women from their husbands — then Abraham answered: I did not know you; rather, I thought, surely there is no fear of Elohim in this place, for in most places of the world there is no fear of Elohim. Therefore, from the time I left my land and went among the nations like a wandering man, not knowing to which place I would come, I stipulated with her to say thus in every place, for the matter is true, and I reckoned in it the saving of a life. And I did not initiate the matter when I came into your land, for I saw no offense in you for which to fear sin. <b>And moreover she is indeed my sister, my father's daughter</b> — another argument. He said: I spoke according to my usual way, which is true; and I reckoned, if perchance they desire her, they will ask me whether she is also my wife. Since your servants took her and asked me nothing, I said, in this place too there is no fear of Elohim, and I kept silent. And it is possible that this stipulation of his with her was made when they came to Egypt, even though he said, \"when Elohim caused me to wander from my father's house\" — or he again urged her there in Egypt at the time of the event, as I explained (Ramban on Genesis 12:11). And the opinion of Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] is that all these words are merely to deflect Avimelekh:"], 